{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":60000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"afb5ec9d5699eb72b6512e6420d553137d3ad9378998c84e22db2a11450bbb68","packTokens":5454,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"platform","tokens":215,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":1850,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"taste","tokens":1054,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"field-ideas","tokens":188,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"reqs","tokens":2114,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":16,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"design"},"pointers":[],"system":"PLATFORM. This product is designed and built as a web app, and nothing else. Where the requirements ask for a mobile app, a phone app, an iOS or Android app, treat them as the same features in one responsive web app that works well on a phone's browser: never stop, park, ask or leave a requirement out because it names a mobile app, and never plan a native app, an app store step or a phone-only frame. Every page is responsive: it is laid out for a phone, a tablet and a desktop width, with touch-sized controls on the narrow one. What only a native app can do (push notifications, background location, a home-screen icon) is given its closest web form (an in-app notice or email, the browser's location prompt, an installable web app) and said so.\n\nYou list the screens of a product for Google Stitch to draw, one prompt per screen.\n- One screen per page a user sees: ids S-1, S-2, ..., each with its route, a proposed file path and the requirement ids it serves in \"reqs\".\n- Every requirement is on a screen, or in \"noScreen\" with the reason it has no page of its own.\n- Each \"prompt\" describes that page alone, in its normal state (its other states are drawn apart, from \"states\"): its purpose, its sections in order, and real sample content from the product's domain (names, amounts, dates). No lorem ipsum, no generic names, nothing the requirements do not ask for.\n- \"links\" lists every way from this page to another screen: its menu items and the buttons and links that open one, each with the exact words on the control (\"label\") and the screen id it opens (\"to\"). A screen has one label, the same on every page that links to it; a button that stays on its page is not listed. The prompt uses those same words for those controls. The pages one kind of user sees share one menu: the same items, labels and order on each of them, and never a page meant for another kind of user.\n- Where a page shows something its user knows by sight (a product, a place, a person, a vehicle), the prompt asks for a real photograph of it, with what is in the picture; never an illustration, an icon in its place or a grey box.\n- \"field-ideas\", when given, tells how the leading real products of this field arrange their pages. Where a requirement fits one of their patterns, the prompt asks for that component by what it is and what it holds (a boarding-pass strip with the route set large, a balance in large tabular figures above the latest movements), with this product's own content. Never a product's name, logo or wording, and never a colour from that list: the design system sets the colours.\n- \"client-references\", when given, are the client's own references with their pictures (R-1, R-2, ...). Stitch never sees them, so the prompt says in words what is taken: for a \"layout\" or \"match\" reference, the navigation and the regions in the order its picture shows, on the screens it relates to; for an \"inspire\" reference, its feel (how dense, how much white, how large the pictures and headings). The requirements beat a reference.\n- \"shells\" lists the frame each kind of user's pages share, one per kind (ids such as \"employee\", \"admin\"). Its \"header\" says in two or three sentences what the header holds, the same on every page: the brand's words as written, where the menu sits (beside the brand or centred), and what is on the right (the signed-in user's name and role, say; whether their picture is a photograph or initials, and on which side of the name it sits), and the brand's mark in one phrase (a small square in the accent with a white \"P\", or the words alone). Each screen names its shell in \"shell\"; a page outside any (sign-in) leaves it out. Every prompt of a shell is given that header word for word, so a prompt never describes the header itself.\n- \"sample\" is the product's sample data, the same on every page: each person with their name, department and role, what the pages show of them (their space, their vehicle and its plate), the place's one time zone, and the counts more than one page shows (capacity per floor, totals), all agreeing with each other, and, when any page lists events (a log, a history, a report, a check), one dated list of them (when, who, what, where, the result) that those pages all take their rows from, with counts that add up to it. Every prompt takes its names, figures and dates from it and never contradicts it; one person is never two people's photograph.\n- Labels, headings and messages use the plain words a user of this product would say (\"Give up my space today\", \"3 spaces free\"), never system or legal wording (\"relinquish\", \"allocation governance\", \"materialize\"). Each page has one primary action; one that cannot be undone says so beside its button.\n- \"title\" is the page's one heading. A page the menu opens has its menu label as its title, word for word; a menu label names a place (\"My space\", \"Free spaces\"), never an action, in three words at most.\n- A page never repeats its menu as a panel of links or tiles. A row that opens a record is itself the link, or has one control that does; no checkbox or radio on a row unless the page acts on several rows at once. Filters apply as they are chosen, with no Apply button. A page shows no field, id, switch, building, gate or device that neither the requirements nor \"sample\" name.\n- \"phone\": true on the pages a person opens on a phone on the move (the main tasks of the largest group of users), five at most; each is drawn a second time at phone width.\n- Each prompt composes its page like a finished product, not a wireframe: one thing the eye meets first (named, with its size against the rest), then the sections in the order the user needs them, what sits beside what on a wide screen, and where the page is left empty on purpose. Give real figures, names and dates enough to fill every list and table with believable rows.\n- \"taste\" holds the layout rules, the anti-patterns and the component replacements the design system is written by. Compose each page by them: where a page would have a component from the left column of its table, the prompt asks for the one on the right, with this product's content (one headline figure with its context above a dense table, never four cards of figures with sparklines; a list or a table, never a grid of near-equal tiles; no row of three equal cards). Its lines about colours, fonts, shadows, motion and CSS are the design system's and stay out of a prompt, and its lines about heroes, pricing, testimonials and footers apply to a public marketing page alone. A page outside any shell that holds one task (sign in, a confirmation, a message that access is refused) is not a hero: its heading, its one sentence and its one action sit in a single panel no wider than 440 px, centred on the page both ways, with the brand's mark and words above the panel and nothing else on the page (no header bar, no footer, no office or place name). The requirements, and a rule above, win over it.\n- \"states\" lists the page's extra states besides its normal page (empty, loading, error, success, validation) only where the requirements need them.\n- \"brand\" lists the client's colours (hex) and fonts only when the requirements or references name them; otherwise leave it empty.\n\n### 6. Define Layout Principles\n- No overlapping elements — every element occupies its own clear spatial zone. No absolute-positioned content stacking\n- Centered Hero sections are BANNED when variance exceeds 4 — force Split Screen, Left-Aligned, or Asymmetric Whitespace\n- The generic \"3 equal cards horizontally\" feature row is BANNED — use 2-column Zig-Zag, asymmetric grid, or horizontal scroll\n- CSS Grid over Flexbox math — never use `calc()` percentage hacks\n- Contain layouts using max-width constraints (e.g., 1400px centered)\n- Full-height sections must use `min-h-[100dvh]` — never `h-screen` (iOS Safari catastrophic jump)\n\n### 9. List Anti-Patterns (AI Tells)\nEncode these as explicit \"NEVER DO\" rules in the DESIGN.md:\n- No emojis anywhere\n- No `Inter` font\n- No generic serif fonts (`Times New Roman`, `Georgia`, `Garamond`) — distinctive modern serifs only if needed\n- No pure black (`#000000`)\n- No neon/outer glow shadows\n- No oversaturated accents\n- No excessive gradient text on large headers\n- No custom mouse cursors\n- No overlapping elements — clean spatial separation always\n- No 3-column equal card layouts\n- No generic names (\"John Doe\", \"Acme\", \"Nexus\")\n- No fake round numbers (`99.99%`, `50%`)\n- No AI copywriting clichés (\"Elevate\", \"Seamless\", \"Unleash\", \"Next-Gen\")\n- No filler UI text: \"Scroll to explore\", \"Swipe down\", scroll arrows, bouncing chevrons\n- No broken Unsplash links — use `picsum.photos` or SVG avatars\n- No centered Hero sections (for high-variance projects)\n\n## 12. Anti-Vibe-Coded Components (extends §5 and §9)\n\nThese are the components that make an app look generated. For each, the DESIGN.md must specify the substitute.\n\n| AI-default component | Replace with |\n|---|---|\n| Pill badge above hero (\"New: AI-powered ✨\") | Nothing, or one dated plain-text line in mono (e.g. \"v2.4 — 3 Oct\") |\n| Feature cards: icon in rounded square + title + 2 lines | Numbered editorial list with real product crops, or annotated screenshot callouts |\n| \"Trusted by\" grayscale logo strip | One specific quote with full name, role and context — or omit |\n| Stats row \"10K+ / 99.9% / 24/7\" | One real metric with its source and timeframe |\n| 3-tier pricing with highlighted \"Most Popular\" middle | Plan comparison table with honest limits, or a single plan with usage-based detail |\n| Testimonial carousel with stars and circular avatars | 1–2 long quotes, set typographically large, no stars |\n| Gradient mesh / blob / glow backgrounds | Flat surface; structure through hairline rules, type scale and spacing |\n| Glassmorphism cards (`backdrop-blur` + white/10) | Solid surfaces with a defined border or a tint step |\n| Bento grid of near-equal tiles | Only when content genuinely differs in size; otherwise a list or table |\n| Dashboard: 4 KPI cards each with sparkline and green \"+12%\" | One headline metric with context, then a dense, sortable table |\n| Hero split: headline left, floating phone/3D render right | The product's actual UI, cropped tight to the interesting part |\n| Sparkle/wand icon for anything \"AI\" | A label that says what the feature does |\n| \"Get started\" / \"Start for free\" CTA | A product-specific verb (\"Plan your first route\", \"Import a spreadsheet\") |\n| Default shadcn look (zinc, 0.5rem radius, ring) on everything | A deliberate radius system (see §13) and a custom focus style |\n| Icon on every list item and nav link | Icons only where they speed up recognition; text labels carry meaning |\n| Mega footer with 4 link columns + newsletter box | Compact footer sized to the real number of links |\n| Uniform section template repeated down the page | Vary section rhythm: full-bleed, narrow text column, dense table, quote |","tools":[],"user":"How the leading products of this field arrange their pages:\nHR and workforce:\n- Workday: plain bar, soft corners; blue on white, orange accent, cards and worklists\n- BambooHR: plain bar, round corners; friendly green, people photos, soft cards\n- Gusto: plain bar, round corners; warm coral on white, plain-language payroll steps\n- ADP: plain bar, sharp corners; red on white, formal tables and pay statements\nWhat their pages share: Friendly but orderly: white pages, a single warm or blue brand colour, people avatars, status chips for pending, approved and rejected, and step-by-step flows for onboarding and payroll. Admin lists are tables with filters.\n\n<artifact id=\"reqs\" kind=\"requirements\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide email-and-password sign-in, send the submitted credentials to the sign-in service, display the resulting landing page or authentication error, render navigation and actions according to the signed-in user's returned permissions, and clear authenticated page data and return to sign-in when an authenticated session expires or a protected request returns HTTP 401.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"ears\": \"Where mock procurement and receiving import is available, the feedlot web app shall let operationally authorized users preview and submit selected records through the documented mock-data adapter and display the returned import result and source fields.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall let operationally authorized users map PO lines to one or more lots and lots to one or more pens, retain allocated animal quantities, and display the resulting traceability and pen information.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"ears\": \"When an operational user repeats an import containing previously imported source-system identifiers, the feedlot web app shall display the import result for the matched records returned by the import service without presenting them as newly created duplicates, and flag returned allocation conflicts when an updated head count is below existing mapped quantities.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-5\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display market-date and target-weight forecasts from incoming animal data and configured yard defaults, use lot-specific overrides when present, show a forecast as unavailable without a positive gain rate, and expose forecast-assumption editing only to authorized managers.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-6\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display feed inventory items and stock movements and let authorized inventory users maintain items, record receipts or adjustments, and change wastage settings, while displaying inventory details to users with viewing access.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display inventory projections for daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, semi-annual, and annual horizons using saved upcoming feed plans and configured wastage, while presenting known actual wastage separately from delivered-feed consumption.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall let authorized ration managers create, revise, and optimize ration definitions, display ingredient proportions, nutrition and cost values, and present the lowest-cost candidate mix meeting user-selected nutritional requirements without saving it until the user chooses to do so.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall let authorized planners generate or adjust dated pen-level feed plans from bunk rates, ration assignments, and animal counts, and display the returned plan details and execution status.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-10\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall flag a saved plan for review when relevant inputs change, retain its saved values until an authorized planner confirms an update or regenerates it, allow assigned-pen operators to update execution status while withholding enabled status-update actions from other operators and displaying returned refusals for rejected updates, and show intake variance without automatically rewriting the saved plan.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-11\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall let operational users record feeding activity by pen and lot, require delivered quantities entered for each lot in a multi-lot pen to add up to the pen's delivered quantity, display the returned variance, cost, energy, inventory consumption, feeding status, and lot-level consumption and cost totals, display a returned stock error when insufficient stock prevents saving, provide permission-controlled correction and void actions for saved feeding events, and display a timed-out save outcome as unknown while leaving retry resolution to the existing service/user workflow.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-12\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall let authorized livestock or operations users add lot weight measurements and display each lot's measurement history as a time-ordered series with returned measurement and gain information.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-13\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display returned lot feed-to-gain metrics for days on feed, total weight gain, average daily gain, total feed consumed in pounds, feed conversion ratio, cost of gain, and projected ship date, and refresh displayed metrics after feeding or weight records change.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-14\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall present explainable rule-based suggestions for feeding adjustments or sale timing based on lot weight-gain trends and leave any resulting plan or sale change to an authorized planner's explicit action.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-15\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display a dashboard of yard and lot performance, inventory, feeding, weight, cost, and energy values and let users select the measure used to rank best- and worst-performing lots.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-16\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide filterable operational reports for inventory, feeding, lot performance, and profitability that answer the requested pen, lot, ration, plan-variance, inventory, and cost-of-gain questions and export the filtered results as CSV.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-17\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display dashboards, reports, and exports using only records and profitability fields returned for the signed-in user's authorized yards and permissions, and remove previously displayed data from view and subsequent exports when returned permissions change.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-18\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide a feed-to-gain report comparing lot ADG, FCR, and cost of gain, with date-range, breed, sex, and pen filters, and CSV or PDF exports of filtered results subject to the user's returned permissions.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-19\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide a filterable on-screen lot profitability list for authorized yards, display service-returned ROI percentages (net return divided by investment) or an unavailable value when ROI is not returned, and display a CSV file containing the filtered authorized results when the user exports the list.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-20\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall let an authorized user load visibly distinct sample data, prevent duplicate sample loading while displaying the returned result, require confirmation before sample-data reset or replacement, distinguish sample records from operational records, exclude sample records from operational report and inventory-total views and their exports, and display dashboard and yard-summary aggregate values as returned by the service.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-21\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall display a yard-level summary of active-lot total head, average ADG, average cost of gain, and total feed cost using returned aggregate values, and show unavailable values when required source data is missing.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-22\",\n  \"ears\": \"The feedlot web app shall provide a delivery overview or presentation page containing the proposed solution, architecture diagrams, app demo entry point, prompts used, testing and validation overview, challenges, production-readiness assessment, and go-to-market analysis.\"\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\nList every screen and write one Stitch prompt for each."}